In this section, we utilize the elliptic curve connections as shown in equation \eqref{2.115} to develop families of infinitely many solutions to the sums of three cubes problem particularly for the unsolved cases of $k = 3, 51$ using group law formulas from elliptic curve theory.

Let us first recall these group laws for Weierstrass elliptic curves:

\subsection{Group Law Formulas}

For an elliptic curve $E: y^2 = x^3 + Ax + B$:

\subsubsection{Point Doubling: $P + P = 2P$}

Let $P = (x_1, y_1)$ be a point on $E$ with $y_1 \neq 0$. Then $2P = (x_3, y_3)$ is computed as:

\begin{align*}
\lambda &= \frac{3x_1^2 + A}{2y_1} \\
x_3 &= \lambda^2 - 2x_1 \\
y_3 &= \lambda(x_1 - x_3) - y_1
\end{align*}

\subsubsection{Point Addition: $P + Q = R$} 
For $P = (x_1, y_1)$, $Q = (x_2, y_2)$ with $x_1 \neq x_2$:
\begin{align*}
\lambda &= \frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1} \\
x_3 &= \lambda^2 - x_1 - x_2 \\
y_3 &= \lambda(x_1 - x_3) - y_1
\end{align*}

\subsubsection*{Special Cases}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Identity: $P + \mathcal{O} = P$
    \item Inverse: $-P = (x_1, -y_1)$  
    \item Vertical: $P + (-P) = \mathcal{O}$
\end{itemize}

