\subsubsection{Modular analysis}

We make a modular analysis (with the computational assistance of SageMath) of the fraction $\dfrac{36n^3 - 19}{\alpha}$ to determine values of $(n, \alpha)$ for which this fraction yields integer solutions. It was determined that $n \equiv a\pmod{p}$ where $a,p$ are positive integers. Within the range $p < 20$, for instance, $n \equiv 4\pmod{5}$, $n \equiv 10\pmod{11}$, $n \equiv 1\pmod{17}$, and $n \equiv 0\pmod{19}$ \cite{Agbanwa_s3c_particularly_for_114}.
Further searches found a promising congruence $n \equiv 2094194294170672539119 \pmod{3782234599225650235415}$, equivalently $n = 3782234599225650235415 \cdot k_1 +  2094194294170672539119$ \cite{agbanwa_congruence_screening}.

Recall that
\begin{equation*}
    m = \dfrac{r^3 - k}{6}
\end{equation*}
For the specific case of $k=114$:
\begin{equation*}
    m = \dfrac{r^3 - 114}{6}
\end{equation*}
Solutions to the above equation are of the form $m = 36n^3 - 19$ and $r = 6n$.

Equation \eqref{2.113} can be rewritten as:

\begin{equation}\label{5.98}
     P^{2} = -Q^{3} + 36n^2 \cdot Q^2 - 2(6n)(36n^3 - 19)  \cdot Q + (36n^3 - 19)^2
\end{equation}

For arbitrary values of $n \geq 1$, we calculate their corresponding rational points $(Q,P)$.

Recall from Section~\ref{sec:derivation} that $Q = 2q$ and that $m = (r^3-k)/6$. Given a rational point $(Q, P)$ on the elliptic curve corresponding to parameter $n$ (so $m = 36n^3-19$, $r = 6n$), the corresponding rational solution $(x, y, z)$ to $x^3+y^3+z^3 = 114$ is recovered via:

\begin{equation}\label{recovery}
    x = \frac{m + P}{Q}, \quad y = \frac{m - P}{Q}, \quad z = r - \frac{2m}{Q}.
\end{equation}

These follow from equations \eqref{1.31}, \eqref{1.32}, and \eqref{3.2}, noting the symmetry of the equation in $x$ and $y$ under relabelling $x_1 \leftrightarrow y_1$.

\subsubsection{\texorpdfstring{Solving equation \eqref{2.113} for $n \equiv 1\pmod{17}$}{Solving equation 2.113 for n equivalent to 1 mod 17}}

When $n = 1$, the resulting equation is:

\begin{equation}\label{5.991}
    P^2 = -Q^3 + 36\cdot Q^2 - 204 \cdot Q + 289
\end{equation}

whose rational point is $(Q,P) = (-6, -55)$ \cite{agbanwa_elliptic_arbitrary_2024}.

Using the recovery formulas \eqref{recovery}:

\begin{align*}
    x &= \dfrac{(36(1)^3 - 19) + (-55)}{-6}
      = \dfrac{19}{3}\\
    y &= \dfrac{(36(1)^3 - 19) - (-55)}{-6}
      = -12\\
    z &= 6(1)- \dfrac{2 \cdot (36(1)^3 - 19)}{-6}
      = \dfrac{35}{3}
\end{align*}

\begin{equation*}
    \left(\dfrac{19}{3}\right)^3 + (-12)^3 + \left(\dfrac{35}{3}\right)^3 = 114
\end{equation*}

Equation \eqref{5.991} is subsequently transformed to this form:

\begin{equation}\label{5.997}
    P^2 = X^3 + 36\cdot X^2 + 204 \cdot X + 289
\end{equation}

with reference to equation \eqref{2.115} where $Q \rightarrow -X$.

Other multiples of the above rational solution can be found computationally using Magma \cite{agbanwa_modulo_17}. The code can be tweaked to generate as many multiples as needed.


